// Getgud SAT Practice Test 2 — a committed, hand-reviewed selection of
// College Board question-bank ids. Generated by
// `pnpm design:exam --exam=sat-practice-2 --seed=getgud-sat-practice-2`
// against the live SAT question bank, then reviewed against this file's
// `spec` blocks and against sat-practice-1.ts's design (see below for what's
// intentionally the same vs. different) before being committed here.
// Re-verify with `pnpm design:exam --exam=sat-practice-2 --verify` after any
// manual edit to an id list.
//
// Because every id here is a College Board natural key (see the header
// comment in ./index.ts), this exact test runs identically on any
// self-hosted instance whose question bank includes these ids.
//
// Same as sat-practice-1 (official digital SAT blueprint, unchanged):
//   - Module lengths (27/27 RW, 22/22 Math), timing, break, routing
//     thresholds, scaled-score tables (same method — see scoring.ts), and
//     per-module domain counts/shares (see design-exam.ts's AF-derived
//     comments).
//   - RW layout: domains ordered Craft & Structure -> Information & Ideas ->
//     Standard English Conventions -> Expression of Ideas, ascending
//     scoreBand within each domain. Math layout: no domain grouping,
//     ascending scoreBand across the whole module, SPR items interleaved
//     near the proportional 1/4, 1/2, 3/4, and final positions.
//   - Module 1 and Module 2 Lower band curves, in both sections, are
//     identical to sat-practice-1's — the routing instrument isn't being
//     second-guessed by a difficulty change underneath it.
//
// Deliberately different, based on informal 2026 test-day recap themes
// (vendor/tutor "post-mortems" and aggregated r/SAT test-day reconstructions
// — student-memory reconstructions and vendor interpretation, NOT official
// College Board data, NOT verified content, and explicitly form-dependent —
// see scripts/design-exam.ts's DESIGNS["sat-practice-2"] comment for the
// specific per-domain targets and reasoning):
//   - Module 2 Upper's band curve, in both sections, is hardened relative to
//     sat-practice-1 — "the real test feels harder than Bluebook, especially
//     Module 2" was the one pattern recurring across many independent 2026
//     test dates, unlike single-form anomalies this design doesn't chase.
//   - The skill mix within RW's Craft & Structure (vocabulary/Words in
//     Context weighted over Text Structure/Cross-Text Connections) and
//     Expression of Ideas (Transitions weighted over Rhetorical Synthesis),
//     and within Math's Algebra (mild tilt toward linear functions/systems)
//     and Advanced Math (heavy tilt toward nonlinear functions/equations),
//     is tilted toward the skills those recaps repeatedly single out. Every
//     other domain (RW's Information & Ideas/Standard English Conventions,
//     Math's Problem-Solving & Data Analysis/Geometry & Trigonometry) keeps
//     the same rarity-based natural spread sat-practice-1 uses everywhere —
//     the recaps gave no clear per-skill signal there, or too few slots (3)
//     to sub-target meaningfully.
//
// Explicitly NOT modeled, because nothing in this app's data model carries
// it: passage genre (science vs. narrative fiction), the specific
// "phrasal/clause" transition-question format, and literal vocabulary words.
// These were the recaps' other headline claims, but there's no `Question`/
// `QuestionAssessment` field to select on for any of them.
//
// `routingThreshold` is a raw-count stand-in for College Board's real
// IRT-theta routing statistic — see scoring.ts's file comment.

